---The client's label, such as studio. Two clients can share a label.
---@field label? string
---The program's name, such as Rex.app or rex.
---@field name? string
---What the client runs on: macos, ios, linux, windows, freebsd, web, or other. Left out if the client didn't say. Treat a platform you don't know as other.
---@field platform? string
---The program's version, as the program prints it, such as 1.6.0.
---@field version? string

---Who the client is, as the server identified it when it accepted the connection.
---@class RexClientListResultClientsItemPrincipal
---A readable name for the client, such as uid 501 or alice@example.com. It can be out of date or shared, so compare uid, sid, or user_id instead.
---@field display string
---The client's Unix group ID. Only for kind peer.
---@field gid? nil|integer
---How the server identified the client: 'peer' (Unix socket credentials), 'windows_peer' (named pipe credentials), 'tailscale' (a Tailscale login), or 'anonymous' (not identified). Show a kind you don't know by its name.
---@field kind string
---The client's Tailscale login name, usually an email address. For display only.
---@field login_name? string
---The name of the client's Tailscale device. Only for kind tailscale.
---@field node? string
---The route the client took to this server, for troubleshooting. Entries include 'tailscale' (built-in Tailscale), 'tailnet' and 'tailnet-serve' (the machine's own Tailscale), and 'login-server' (passed on by a login server).
