---@class RexServerAppleGrantFileAccessArgs
---The files to make readable, in the order they were dropped or pasted. At most 256.
---@field files nil|RexServerAppleGrantFileAccessArgsFilesItem[]

---@class RexServerAppleGrantFileAccessArgsFilesItem
---The file's absolute path. If it names a directory, access covers everything inside it.
---@field path string
---A macOS sandbox extension token for path, which the app gets from sandbox_extension_issue_file. The server uses it only if it can't already open path.
---@field token? string

---@class RexServerAppleGrantFileAccessResult
---One result for each requested file, in the same order as the request.
---@field files nil|RexServerAppleGrantFileAccessResultFilesItem[]

---@class RexServerAppleGrantFileAccessResultFilesItem
---'readable' if the server could already open the file, 'granted' if it can now because it used the token, or 'denied' if it still can't. Treat a value you don't know as 'denied'.
---@field access string
---Why access was denied, written for a log. The wording can change, so check access instead.
---@field message? string
---The requested path, unchanged.
---@field path string

---@class RexServerApplePrivacyStatusArgs

---@class RexServerApplePrivacyStatusResult
---The executable macOS holds responsible for the server. Privacy decisions belong to the app that contains it. Left out when the server can't tell.
---@field responsible_path? string
---One entry for each privacy service the server knows. Empty when the server can't read privacy decisions, for example when it doesn't run on macOS.
---@field services nil|RexServerApplePrivacyStatusResultServicesItem[]

---@class RexServerApplePrivacyStatusResultServicesItem
---The protected resource. Later versions of Rex may add services, so skip one you don't know.
---@field service string
---'granted' if the server may use the resource, 'denied' if the person turned it off or has not turned it on, 'not_determined' if the person has not been asked yet, or 'unknown' if the server could not tell. Treat a value you don't know as 'unknown'.
---@field status string

---@class RexServerConfigReloadArgs

---@class RexServerConfigReloadResult
---Problems found in the configuration file, written for people to read. Empty when there are none.
---@field diagnostics nil|RexServerConfigReloadResultDiagnosticsItem[]
---How many times the server has loaded its configuration file since it started, counting this load. It goes up on every load, even one that fails.
---@field generation integer

---@class RexServerConfigReloadResultDiagnosticsItem
---The full name of the action the problem is about, as written, even if misspelled. Left out if it isn't about one action.
---@field action? string
---The key the problem is about, spelled like a binding's keys, with a sequence joined by spaces, such as ctrl+a b. Left out if it isn't about one key.
---@field key? string
---The problem in one sentence, ready to show. Its wording may change, so don't parse it.
---@field message string
---The mode the problem is about. Left out if it isn't about a mode.
---@field mode? string
---How serious the problem is: warning, for something the user probably didn't mean, or error, for a part that was skipped. Show a value you don't know as a warning.
---@field severity string
---Where the problem is, as file:line, such as init.lua:22. Left out when it isn't known.
---@field source? string

---@class RexServerStatusArgs

---@class RexServerStatusResult
---The oldest revision of the API the server supports. Left out when it is 0.
---@field api_min_revision? integer
---The newest revision of the API the server supports. Left out when it is 0.
---@field api_revision? integer
---The configuration file the server loaded most recently. Always present, even when there is no file.
---@field config RexServerStatusResultConfig
---True if the server stops on its own soon after its last session and last connection close.
---@field exit_empty boolean
---Identifies the machine and account the server runs as. Servers that see the same files report the same value. Left out when the server has no state directory.
---@field host_id? string
---A random ID for this run of the server. A new value means a new server process, even if pid is the same.
---@field instance_id string
---The server's process ID.
---@field pid integer
---How many sessions the server has.
---@field session_count integer
---When the server started, as an RFC 3339 timestamp.
---@field started_at string
---The status of built-in Tailscale. Present only when the server can run it.
---@field tailscale? RexServerStatusResultTailscale

---The configuration file the server loaded most recently. Always present, even when there is no file.
---@class RexServerStatusResultConfig
---How many times the server has loaded its configuration file since it started, counting from 1. It goes up on every load, even one that fails.
---@field generation integer
---The SHA-256 of the configuration file and the modules it requires while it loads, in lowercase hexadecimal. Empty if there's no file or it couldn't be read.
---@field hash string

---The status of built-in Tailscale. Present only when the server can run it.
---@class RexServerStatusResultTailscale
---The Tailscale account that signed the device in. Left out until Tailscale reports it.
---@field account? RexServerStatusResultTailscaleAccount
---True if anyone your tailnet's access rules allow may connect. False lets in only the Tailscale user who signed the device in.
---@field allow_tailnet_members boolean
---The link to open to sign the device in to Tailscale. Present while state is needs_login, once Tailscale has made the link.
---@field auth_url? string
---The control server the device uses, such as a Headscale server. Left out for Tailscale's own, while built-in Tailscale is off, and by older servers.
---@field control_url? string
---The device's MagicDNS name, such as rex.tail1234.ts.net. Left out until known.
---@field dns_name? string
---Why built-in Tailscale turned off after its HTTPS listener failed. The next enable clears it.
---@field error? string
---The state of the device's HTTPS listener. Left out while built-in Tailscale is off, and by older servers.
---@field https? RexServerStatusResultTailscaleHttps
---'disabled' (off), 'starting', 'needs_login' (see auth_url), 'needs_machine_approval' (a tailnet admin must approve the device), or 'running' (on the tailnet). Treat a value you don't know as 'starting'.
---@field state string
---The Tailscale tags the device has, such as tag:rex. A tagged device has no owner. Left out when it has none.
---@field tags? nil|string[]
---The tailnet's display name, or its domain if it has none. Left out until known.
---@field tailnet_name? string

---The Tailscale account that signed the device in. Left out until Tailscale reports it.
---@class RexServerStatusResultTailscaleAccount
---The Tailscale login name, usually an email address. For display only, because it can change.
---@field login_name string

---The state of the device's HTTPS listener. Left out while built-in Tailscale is off, and by older servers.
---@class RexServerStatusResultTailscaleHttps
---'pending' until the HTTPS listener is ready, then 'available'. The server is reachable over the tailnet only when available. Treat a value you don't know as 'pending'.
---@field state string
---The server's address on the tailnet, such as https://rex.tail1234.ts.net. Present once HTTPS is available and the device's name is known.
---@field url? string

---@class RexServerStopArgs
---Stop only if the server has no sessions and no other client is connected. A server that is in use refuses with conflict and keeps running.
---@field if_idle? boolean

---@class RexServerStopResult

